    <h1>Boxing and Unboxing in C#</h1>

    <p>
        Boxing and Unboxing in C# are used to convert value types into reference
        types and reference types back into value types.
    </p>

    <p>
        This mainly happens between:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>Value types (int, char, bool, struct, etc.)</li>
        <li>Reference type (object)</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        In C#:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>Value types are generally stored in stack memory.</li>
        <li>Reference types are stored in heap memory.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>Boxing</h2>

    <p>
        Boxing is the process of converting a value type into a reference type.
    </p>

    <p>
        During boxing:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>The value is copied from stack to heap memory.</li>
        <li>An object is created in heap memory.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Boxing Example</h3>

    <pre>
int num = 100;

object obj = num;
    </pre>

    <p>
        Here:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>num</strong> is a value type.</li>
        <li>It gets converted into an <strong>object</strong> type.</li>
        <li>This process is called Boxing.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>Memory Representation</h2>

    <h3>Before Boxing</h3>

    <pre>
Stack:
num = 100
    </pre>

    <h3>After Boxing</h3>

    <pre>
Stack:
obj -----> Heap Object(100)
    </pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>Unboxing</h2>

    <p>
        Unboxing is the process of converting a boxed object back into a value type.
    </p>

    <p>
        During unboxing:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>Data is extracted from the heap object.</li>
        <li>The value is converted back into its original datatype.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Unboxing Example</h3>

    <pre>
object obj = 100;

int num = (int)obj;
    </pre>

    <p>
        Here:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>obj</strong> contains boxed integer value.</li>
        <li>The value is extracted using explicit type casting.</li>
        <li>This process is called Unboxing.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>Complete Example</h2>

    <pre>
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        int number = 100;

        // Boxing
        object obj = number;

        Console.WriteLine(obj);

        // Unboxing
        int value = (int)obj;

        Console.WriteLine(value);
    }
}
    </pre>

    <h3>Output</h3>

    <pre>
100
100
    </pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>Real-world Example - ArrayList</h2>

    <p>
        Before Generics, collections like <strong>ArrayList</strong> stored values
        as <strong>object</strong> type.
    </p>

    <pre>
using System;
using System.Collections;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        ArrayList list = new ArrayList();

        // Boxing
        list.Add(10);
        list.Add(true);
        list.Add('A');

        // Unboxing
        int number = (int)list[0];

        Console.WriteLine(number);
    }
}
    </pre>

    <h3>Output</h3>

    <pre>
10
    </pre>

    <p>
        Here:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>Values are automatically boxed when added to ArrayList.</li>
        <li>Values are unboxed when retrieved using type casting.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>Why Boxing and Unboxing Matter?</h2>

    <p>
        Boxing and unboxing affect:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>Performance</li>
        <li>Memory usage</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Boxing creates objects in heap memory which increases garbage collection.
    </p>

    <p>
        Excessive boxing and unboxing can reduce application performance.
    </p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Performance-friendly Alternative</h2>

    <p>
        Generics help avoid boxing and unboxing.
    </p>

    <h3>Bad Practice</h3>

    <pre>
ArrayList list = new ArrayList();
    </pre>

    <h3>Better Practice</h3>

    <pre>
List&lt;int&gt; list = new List&lt;int&gt;();
    </pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>Difference Between Boxing and Unboxing</h2>

    <table border="1" cellpadding="10">
        <tr>
            <th>Boxing</th>
            <th>Unboxing</th>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Value type to reference type</td>
            <td>Reference type to value type</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Implicit conversion</td>
            <td>Explicit conversion</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Stored in heap memory</td>
            <td>Extracted back to value type</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Creates object</td>
            <td>Requires type casting</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <hr>

    <h2>Important Points</h2>

    <ul>
        <li>Boxing converts value type into object type.</li>
        <li>Unboxing converts object type back into value type.</li>
        <li>Boxing is implicit.</li>
        <li>Unboxing requires explicit type casting.</li>
        <li>Excessive boxing and unboxing reduce performance.</li>
        <li>Generics help avoid boxing and unboxing.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>Interview Definition</h2>

    <p>
        Boxing in C# is the process of converting a value type into a reference
        type (<strong>object</strong>). Unboxing is the process of converting the
        boxed object back into its original value type using explicit type casting.
    </p>

</div>